</figure>

<style>
  /* 1100px is the .wrap content width (1180 less its 40px padding), so the
     drawing fills the column exactly rather than being letterboxed by the
     gutters the callouts need. */
  /* Drawing-space type. The unit here is the viewBox, not the screen, so the
     numbers differ between the two compositions even though the roles are
     identical. Four roles: a label, a name, a figure, and the callout.

     The callout earned its own size on 18 Aug 2026. It is the only text in the
     plate that is the page's content rather than part of the interface being
     depicted — CLAUDE.md calls the six callouts content, not decoration — and
     it is the only one whose rendered size has to hold across the narrow
     composition's whole scale range. Every other role is drawn UI and can ride
     the drawing's scale without anyone minding. */
  .dp-wide   { --fs-micro: 11px; --fs-name: 16px; --fs-fig: 28px; --fs-colabel: 11px; }

  /* Between 821 and 1000 the wide drawing is still in use but the column has
     dropped to 740-920px, so a 1040-unit canvas renders at 0.71-0.89 and the
     names fell to 12.6px against 16.5px body copy. Name and figure are bumped
     to compensate; --fs-colabel deliberately is not, because the callout labels
     sit close to the canvas edge and "Live counts from your tools" overflows it
     above 12 units. (That constraint used to be written against --fs-micro,
     which the wide callout shared until the callout took its own role.) */
  @media (min-width: 821px) and (max-width: 1000px) {
    .dp-wide { --fs-name: 21px; --fs-fig: 34px; }
  }
  /* Capped at its own 440-unit width for the same reason as the hero's narrow
     composition: unbounded, it scaled 1.56x at tablet and rendered its figures
     at 31px.

     The cap is why --fs-colabel is 14 here and 11 in the wide drawing. This
     composition does not render at one scale: a 375px phone gives the SVG
     331px, or 0.75, while anything from 484px up hits the 440 cap at 1.0. So a
     unit value lands at two different sizes. 14 splits the difference, at
     10.5px on a phone and 14px above the cap, against a 10px --text-micro. It
     was 18, inherited from --fs-name, which rendered at 18px above the cap —
     larger than 16.5px body copy, and the size the owner reported. --fs-name is
     absent here because the callout was its only consumer in this composition. */
  .dp-narrow { --fs-micro: 11px; --fs-fig: 24px; --fs-colabel: 14px; max-width: 440px; }

  .dashboard-plate { margin-top: 46px; max-width: 1100px; }
  .dp-plate { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; overflow: visible; }
  .dp-narrow { display: none; }

  /* Type inside the narrow composition is sized against a 440-unit canvas
     rather than a 1040-unit one, so these are roughly 2.4x the wide values
     and land at about the same rendered size on a phone. */
  .dp-url-n     { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-micro); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide); fill: var(--color-faint); }
  .dp-lbl-n     { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-micro); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide); fill: var(--color-faint); }
  .dp-h-n       { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--fs-fig); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight); fill: var(--color-ink); }
  .dp-fig-n     { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--fs-fig); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight); fill: var(--color-ink); }
  .dp-btntext-n { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-micro); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide); fill: var(--color-paper); text-anchor: middle; }
  .dp-colabel-n { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-colabel); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide); text-transform: uppercase; fill: var(--color-body); }

  /* ---- paint. no hex; every value is a token from global.css ---- */
  .dp-panel     { fill: var(--color-white); stroke: var(--color-hair); stroke-width: 1; }
  .dp-card      { fill: var(--color-white); stroke: var(--color-hair); stroke-width: 1; }
  .dp-rule      { stroke: var(--color-rule); stroke-width: 1; fill: none; }
  .dp-dots      { fill: var(--color-hair); }
  .dp-hair      { fill: var(--color-hair); }
  .dp-urlbar    { fill: var(--color-wash); }
  .dp-avatar    { fill: var(--color-wash); stroke: var(--color-hair); stroke-width: 1; }

  /* the client's own mark and colour */
  .dp-mark      { fill: var(--color-koa); }
  .dp-wordmark  { fill: var(--color-koa); }
  .dp-navactive { fill: var(--color-wash); }

  .dp-url       { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-micro); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide); fill: var(--color-faint); }
  .dp-lbl       { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-micro); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide); fill: var(--color-faint); }
  .dp-h         { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--fs-fig); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight); fill: var(--color-ink); }
  .dp-fig       { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--fs-fig); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight); fill: var(--color-ink); }
  .dp-navon     { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--fs-name); fill: var(--color-koa); }
  .dp-navoff    { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--fs-name); fill: var(--color-body); }
  .dp-rowtext   { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--fs-name); fill: var(--color-body); }

  .dp-btn       { fill: var(--color-koa); }
  .dp-btntext   { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-micro); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide); fill: var(--color-paper); text-anchor: middle; }

  /* callouts. The marker reuses ServiceList's numbered-circle treatment
     exactly, at 26px instead of 34px, so no new pattern enters the design. */
  .dp-leader     { stroke: var(--color-line); stroke-width: 1; fill: none; }
  .dp-marker     { fill: var(--color-paper); stroke: var(--color-line); stroke-width: 1; }
  .dp-markertext{ font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-micro); fill: var(--color-koa); text-anchor: middle; }
  .dp-colabel   { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-colabel); letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide); text-transform: uppercase; fill: var(--color-body); }

  .dp-grid path { stroke: var(--color-rule); stroke-width: 1; fill: none; }
  /* Three series, darkest on top. All one hue family, so it reads as one chart
     rather than three competing colours. No fills anywhere in here — see the
     note in the markup. */
  /* stroke-dasharray sits here rather than in the motion block so the value is
     always present; the keyframe only moves the offset. A dasharray of 1 on a
     path normalised to pathLength=1 means one dash covers the whole line. */
  .dp-s1, .dp-s2, .dp-s3 { fill: none; stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-dasharray: 1; }
  .dp-s1 { stroke: var(--color-koa);   stroke-width: 2; }
  .dp-s2 { stroke: var(--color-line);  stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .dp-s3 { stroke: var(--color-ochre); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .dp-point { fill: var(--color-white); stroke: var(--color-koa); stroke-width: 2; }
  .dp-status    { fill: var(--color-line); }
  .dp-status-off{ fill: var(--color-hair); }

  /* ============================================================
     Motion — CSS only. No JavaScript, no library, no SMIL.
     Entry runs once; the plate sits below the fold on most screens but
     above it on a tall desktop, so this is time-driven rather than
     scroll-driven for the same reason HeroGraphic is.
     ============================================================ */
  @keyframes dp-reveal { to { opacity: 1; } }
  @keyframes dp-draw   { to { stroke-dashoffset: 0; } }
  @keyframes dp-rise   { to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } }
  
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
      /* Scroll-driven, not load-driven. This plate is one to two screens below
         the fold on a phone and mostly below it on a laptop, so a reveal that
         started 0.1s after page load finished before anyone saw the drawing.
         Nobody on mobile had ever seen this animation.

         The timeline is named on the figure rather than each element calling
         view() for itself: these are SVG children, and one timeline on the
         container is both more reliable and easier to reason about. Descendants
         find it by walking up the tree.

         Ranges use `cover`, not `entry`. The plate is taller than a phone
         viewport, and `entry 100%` is unreachable for an element that never
         fully fits on screen — the animation would stall part-played.

         Longhands and from-states-in-keyframes throughout: landmine 22. If any
         of this fails to parse, the drawing renders finished rather than
         invisible. */
      .dashboard-plate { view-timeline-name: --plate; }

      .dp-side, .dp-head, .dp-stat, .dp-chart, .dp-list,
      .dp-s1, .dp-s2, .dp-s3, .dp-point, .co {
        animation-timing-function: ease-out;
        animation-fill-mode: both;
        animation-timeline: --plate;
      }

      /* the window and its panels */
      .dp-side  { animation-name: dp-reveal; animation-range: cover 4%  cover 16%; }
      .dp-head  { animation-name: dp-reveal; animation-range: cover 7%  cover 19%; }
      .dp-stat  { animation-name: dp-rise;   animation-range: cover 10% cover 24%; }
      .s2       { animation-range: cover 12% cover 26%; }
      .s3       { animation-range: cover 14% cover 28%; }
      .dp-chart { animation-name: dp-rise;   animation-range: cover 16% cover 30%; }
      .dp-list  { animation-name: dp-rise;   animation-range: cover 18% cover 32%; }

      /* the three series draw back to front, then the current point lands */
      .dp-s3    { animation-name: dp-draw;   animation-range: cover 22% cover 40%; }
      .dp-s2    { animation-name: dp-draw;   animation-range: cover 25% cover 43%; }
      .dp-s1    { animation-name: dp-draw;   animation-range: cover 28% cover 46%; }
      .dp-point { animation-name: dp-reveal; animation-range: cover 46% cover 50%; }

      /* callouts last, once the thing they point at exists */
      .co       { animation-name: dp-reveal; animation-range: cover 34% cover 48%; }
      .c1 { animation-range: cover 36% cover 50%; }
      .c5 { animation-range: cover 38% cover 52%; }
      .c3 { animation-range: cover 40% cover 54%; }
      .c4 { animation-range: cover 42% cover 56%; }
      .c2 { animation-range: cover 44% cover 58%; }
    }
  }

  /* Composition swap, not a scale-down and not a sideways pan. Both drawings
